To compare with .NET 10, target `net10.0;net11.0`, replace `<BasePath />` in `App.razor` with `<base href="/" />` and add `app.UseAntiforgery();` before `app.MapStaticAssets();`. For the QuickGrid page, reference QuickGrid 10.0.0 for `net10.0` and 11.0.0 for `net11.0`.

### Exceptions (if any)

None.

### .NET Version

SDK 11.0.100, runtime 11.0.0 (ASP.NET Core 11.0.0). Compared with ASP.NET Core 10.0.15. Chrome 157 (headless, driven through the DevTools protocol), Windows 11.

### Anything else?

`handleJumpKeys` is unchanged on `main` apart from the added `interruptProgrammaticScroll()` calls.

## Background and Motivation

`Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform` (#69304) gives Blazor applications typed access to standard browser APIs. `Window.Navigator.GetOnLineAsync()` (#69967) reads whether the browser has network access. But an application that shows an offline banner has to poll it today, because the events that report a change are missing.

This proposal adds the `online` and `offline` events of `window`, as the next part of the browser context family (#69872). They use the event listener API from #69922, as the `storage` event of Web Storage does.

The HTML specification defines `navigator.onLine` and these two events together, and web-features lists them as one feature, "Online status". So they belong to the same family as `Window.Navigator`.

`languagechange` also fires on `window`, and #69967 said it would come with these two. It is left out, because neither #69872 nor the Epic (#69987) asks for it. It can be added later in the same way.

The implementation is in #70530.

## Usage Examples

```csharp
builder.Services.AddBrowserPlatform();
```

```razor
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject IBrowserPlatform Browser

@if (!online)
{
    <p class="banner">You are offline. Changes are saved when the connection comes back.</p>
}

@code {
    private bool online = true;
    private IAsyncDisposable? onlineListener;
    private IAsyncDisposable? offlineListener;

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender)
        {
            onlineListener = await Browser.Window.AddEventListenerAsync(Window.OnlineEvent, _ => SetOnline(true));
            offlineListener = await Browser.Window.AddEventListenerAsync(Window.OfflineEvent, _ => SetOnline(false));
            await SetOnline(await Browser.Window.Navigator.GetOnLineAsync());
        }
    }

    private Task SetOnline(bool value)
    {
        online = value;
        StateHasChanged();
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        if (onlineListener is not null)
        {
            await onlineListener.DisposeAsync();
        }

        if (offlineListener is not null)
        {
            await offlineListener.DisposeAsync();
        }
    }
}
```

The API works in Interactive Server and WebAssembly, but it is useful mainly in WebAssembly. In Interactive Server, the listener cannot learn about an offline period in time (see "Risks").

## Proposed API

```diff
 namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform;

+public sealed class Event
+{
+    public Event();
+}

 public static class WindowNavigatorExtensions
 {
+    extension(Window)
+    {
+        public static EventType<Window, Event> OnlineEvent { get; }
+        public static EventType<Window, Event> OfflineEvent { get; }
+    }
+
     extension(Window window)
     {
         public Navigator Navigator { get; }
     }
 }
```

The event keys follow #69922. Each event has one static `EventType<TTarget, TEvent>` property named `<Name>Event`. An event of `Window` is declared in an `extension(Window)` block of its family's extension class, as `Window.StorageEvent` is in `WindowWebStorageExtensions`. `Window.AddEventListenerAsync` already exists.

Both events are a plain `Event` in the HTML specification, with no members of their own. `Event` is the .NET type for such an event, and it has no members for now. The event types of the package copy only the members that their own Web IDL interface declares, so `CookieChangeEvent` and `StorageEvent` have none of the members that every event has, such as `type`. `Event` leaves them out too, so that all event types agree. Members can be added later without a breaking change. Other events that are a plain `Event`, such as the `abort` event of `AbortSignal`, can use the same type.

## Alternative Designs

Applications can keep using `IJSRuntime` with their own JavaScript, or poll `Window.Navigator.GetOnLineAsync()`.

A new extension class only for these two keys was considered. `WindowNavigatorExtensions` was chosen, because the specification and web-features treat `navigator.onLine` and the two events as one feature.

`Event` could copy `type`, so that one listener can serve both events and tell them apart. It does not, so that it stays consistent with `CookieChangeEvent` and `StorageEvent`, which have no `Type`. A listener per event already knows which event it receives.

The keys could be left out of Interactive Server, for example by throwing `NotSupportedException` there. They are not, because every family of the package has the same API in both render modes, and a component in the Interactive Auto render mode runs in both. The XML docs of the two keys describe the limitation instead.

## Risks

`Event` is a general name in the `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform` namespace. It is the Web IDL name, as `StorageEvent` and `CookieChangeEvent` are.

In Interactive Server, the listener runs on the server, and the events are of little use.

- If the connection to the server survives the offline period, the `offline` event reaches the server only after the network comes back, just before the `online` event.
- If the connection is lost, neither event reaches the server. The browser fires `online` before Blazor reconnects, and a call from JavaScript to .NET is not queued while the circuit is disconnected.

Blazor's reconnection UI already tells the user about a lost connection there. WebAssembly has no such limitation.

Like the other events of the package, the API is unavailable before interactivity.

## Source Justifications

- Applications need the online state to adapt their behavior: "Expose browser environment information needed by applications to adapt their behavior." (#69872)
- The online state is part of the browser context family: "Online state" (#69872)
- The events were left out of the Navigator proposal for a later one: "The `online`, `offline`, and `languagechange` events fire on `window`, not on `navigator`. They are not part of this proposal. They will use the events API from #69922 and will be proposed separately." (#69967)
- The browser fires `offline` at `window` when `navigator.onLine` becomes `false`: "When the value that would be returned by the navigator.onLine attribute of a Window or WorkerGlobalScope global changes from true to false, the user agent must queue a global task on the networking task source given global to fire an event named offline at global." ([HTML](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/system-state.html#navigator.online))
- And `online` when it becomes `true`: "when the value that would be returned by the navigator.onLine attribute of a Window or WorkerGlobalScope global changes from false to true, the user agent must queue a global task on the networking task source given global to fire an event named online at the Window or WorkerGlobalScope object." ([HTML](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/system-state.html#navigator.online))
- Both are `Window` events: "`attribute EventHandler onoffline;`", "`attribute EventHandler ononline;`" (`interface mixin WindowEventHandlers` in [html.idl](https://github.com/w3c/webref/blob/main/ed/idl/html.idl))
- `navigator.onLine` and the events are one feature: "The `navigator.onLine` property is a boolean for whether the browser is connected to some network (though not necessarily the internet). The `online` and `offline` events fire when the connection state changes." (`online` in [web-features](https://github.com/web-platform-dx/web-features))
- It is Baseline widely available: "`baseline: high`", "`baseline_high_date: 2018-01-29`" (`online` in [web-features](https://github.com/web-platform-dx/web-features))
- The event types copy only the members of their own interface: "copy only the members that the event interface itself declares" (`.github/skills/port-components-platform-api/SKILL.md`)
- An event of `Window` is a static key in the family's extension class: "`public static EventType<Window, StorageEvent> StorageEvent => _storageEvent;`" (`WindowWebStorageExtensions.cs`)
- The new keys: "`public static EventType<Window, Event> OnlineEvent => _onlineEvent;`", "`public static EventType<Window, Event> OfflineEvent => _offlineEvent;`" (`WindowNavigatorExtensions.cs`)
- The event object copies nothing: "`EventTypes.Create<Window, Event>("online", "[{}]")`" (`WindowNavigatorExtensions.cs`)
- The API works in Interactive Server and WebAssembly: "`NavigatorPaths = ["/navigator-server", "/navigator-wasm"]`" (`NavigatorTests.cs`)
- A call from JavaScript to .NET in Interactive Server is sent on the current connection only: "`this._connection!.send('BeginInvokeDotNetFromJS', callId ? callId.toString() : null, assemblyName, methodIdentifier, dotNetObjectId || 0, argsJson);`" (`src/Components/Web.JS/src/Platform/Circuits/CircuitManager.ts`)
- The XML docs state the limitation: "If the connection to the server was lost while the browser was offline, the event fires before that connection is restored, and the listener does not receive it." (`WindowNavigatorExtensions.cs`)

